+@c Copyright (C) 2026 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
+@c This is part of the GAS manual.
+@c For copying conditions, see the file as.texinfo.
+@c
+@c A "@c doctruth: KIND" comment above an @smallexample marks a block that
+@c the tools really print - a diagnostic, or the instruction list - and
+@c that a check holds to the sources.
+
+@ifset GENERIC
+@page
+@node i51-Dependent
+@chapter Intel MCS-51 (8051) Dependent Features
+@end ifset
+
+@ifclear GENERIC
+@node Machine Dependencies
+@chapter Intel MCS-51 (8051) Dependent Features
+@end ifclear
+
+@cindex MCS-51 support
+@cindex 8051 support
+@menu
+* i51 Options::                 Options
+* i51 Syntax::                  Syntax
+* i51 Byte Order::              Byte Order
+* i51 Floating Point::          Floating Point
+* i51 Memory Spaces::           Memory Spaces
+* i51 Directives::              MCS-51 Machine Directives
+* i51 Opcodes::                 Opcodes
+* i51 Relocations::             Relocations
+@end menu
+
+@node i51 Options
+@section Options
+
+@cindex MCS-51 options
+@cindex options for MCS-51
+@table @gcctabopt
+@cindex @option{-mmcu} command-line option, MCS-51
+@item -mmcu=@var{part}
+The part whose special function register and bit names the assembler knows
+(@pxref{i51-Regs}): @samp{8051}, @samp{8052}, which adds the timer 2
+names, or @samp{at89s8252}, which adds those of the Atmel AT89S8252 as
+well.  The default is @samp{at89s8252}.
+
+@cindex @option{-mcase-sensitive} command-line option, MCS-51
+@item -mcase-sensitive
+Keep symbol names as they are written instead of folding them to upper
+case (@pxref{i51-Case}).
+@end table
+
+There is no @option{-EB} or @option{-EL}: section contents are always big
+endian.  @xref{i51 Byte Order}.
+
+@cindex debugging information, MCS-51
+@option{-g} produces DWARF line information, and @code{.file} and
+@code{.loc} are accepted, so @command{addr2line} and @samp{objdump -l} can
+map code addresses to source lines.  Addresses in debugging information are
+four bytes wide, relocated with @code{R_I51_32}.
+
+@node i51 Syntax
+@section Syntax
+@menu
+* i51-Chars::                   Special Characters
+* i51-Case::                    Case
+* i51-Regs::                    Register and Operand Names
+* i51-Modifiers::               Address Modifiers
+@end menu
+
+@node i51-Chars
+@subsection Special Characters
+
+@cindex line comment character, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 line comment character
+A semicolon (@samp{;}) appearing anywhere on a line starts a comment that
+extends to the end of that line.
+
+If a @samp{#} appears as the first character of a line then the whole line
+is treated as a comment, but it can also be a logical line number directive
+(@pxref{Comments}) or a preprocessor control command
+(@pxref{Preprocessing}).  Anywhere else on a line, @samp{#} introduces an
+immediate operand, as it does in other MCS-51 assemblers:
+@samp{mov a,#10}.
+
+@cindex line separator, MCS-51
+@cindex statement separator, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 line separator
+The @samp{$} character can be used instead of a newline to separate
+statements.
+
+@cindex indirect operands, MCS-51
+The @samp{@@} character introduces an indirect operand, as in
+@samp{@@R0} or @samp{@@DPTR}.  It is part of a fixed operand name rather
+than a prefix that may be applied to an arbitrary expression; the names
+that exist are listed in @ref{i51-Regs}.
+
+@node i51-Case
+@subsection Case
+
+@cindex case insensitivity, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 case insensitivity
+Instruction mnemonics, directive names and operand names may all be written
+in either case: @samp{MOV A,#10} and @samp{mov a,#10} assemble to the same
+two bytes.
+
+@cindex symbol names, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 symbol names
+Symbol names are case insensitive too, unless @option{-mcase-sensitive}
+is given.  @command{@value{AS}} implements that by folding every symbol
+name to upper case as the symbol is created, so @code{buf}, @code{Buf} and
+@code{BUF} all name one symbol, and the name that reaches the object file
+is @code{BUF}.  A name that starts with a dot keeps its case: section
+names, and the symbols made from them such as @code{.startof..text}, are
+case sensitive.  Names defined with
+@option{--defsym} are folded in the same way.  A consequence is that a
+symbol which has to match a name written outside the assembler (in a linker
+script, or in an object produced by another tool) has to be spelled in
+upper case there.
+
+@cindex startup hooks, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 startup hooks
+The linker script this port installs takes the folding into account.  It
+ends @code{.text} with a single @code{RET} instruction, labelled
+@code{__I51_RET}, and provides the three startup hooks
+@code{__GSINIT_STARTUP}, @code{__EXTERNAL_STARTUP} and @code{__INIT_DATA}
+against it, so that a program which supplies no startup routine of its
+own calls that @code{RET} instead of failing the link or aiming the call
+at address zero, the reset vector.  Each of the three is provided under
+its lower-case spelling as well - @code{__gsinit_startup},
+@code{__external_startup} and @code{__init_data} - because only one of
+the two spellings can match a given reference: an object this assembler
+built asks for the upper-case name, while an object from a tool that
+does not fold names asks for the lower-case one.
+
+@node i51-Regs
+@subsection Register and Operand Names
+
+@cindex MCS-51 register names
+@cindex register names, MCS-51
+The assembler knows the operand names below.  A name is recognized in any
+case, and only where the instruction being assembled has a matching operand
+form.
+
+@table @asis
+@item Registers and addressing modes
+@code{A}, @code{AB}, @code{C}, @code{/C}, @code{R0} @dots{} @code{R7},
+@code{@@R0}, @code{@@R1}, @code{DPTR}, @code{@@DPTR}, @code{@@A+DPTR} and
+@code{@@A+PC}.
+
+@item Register-bank byte addresses
+@code{AR0} @dots{} @code{AR7} stand for the byte addresses of @code{R0}
+@dots{} @code{R7} in the register bank selected by the most recent
+@code{.using} directive.  Using one of them without a preceding
+@code{.using} is an error.
+
+@item Byte-addressable special function registers
+@code{P0}, @code{SP}, @code{DPL}, @code{DP0L}, @code{DPH}, @code{DP0H},
+@code{DP1L}, @code{DP1H}, @code{SPDR}, @code{PCON}, @code{TCON},
+@code{TMOD}, @code{TL0}, @code{TL1}, @code{TH0}, @code{TH1}, @code{P1},
+@code{WMCON}, @code{SCON}, @code{SBUF}, @code{P2}, @code{IE}, @code{SPSR},
+@code{P3}, @code{IP}, @code{T2CON}, @code{T2MOD}, @code{RCAP2L},
+@code{RCAP2H}, @code{TL2}, @code{TH2}, @code{PSW}, @code{SPCR},
+@code{ACC} and @code{B}.  Each stands for the register's address in the
+SFR space.
+
+@item Bit-addressable names
+The bits of @code{P0}, @code{P1}, @code{P2} and @code{P3} are
+@code{P0.0} @dots{} @code{P3.7}, and the port 3 bits also have the names
+@code{RXD}, @code{TXD}, @code{INT0}, @code{INT1}, @code{T0}, @code{T1},
+@code{WR} and @code{RD}.  The bits of @code{ACC} and @code{B} are
+@code{ACC.0} @dots{} @code{ACC.7} and @code{B.0} @dots{} @code{B.7}.
+The remaining named bits are @code{IT0}, @code{IE0}, @code{IT1},
+@code{IE1}, @code{TR0}, @code{TF0}, @code{TR1}, @code{TF1} in
+@code{TCON}; @code{T2} and @code{T2EX} in @code{P1}; @code{RI},
+@code{TI}, @code{RB8}, @code{TB8}, @code{REN}, @code{SM2}, @code{SM1},
+@code{SM0} in @code{SCON}; @code{EX0}, @code{ET0}, @code{EX1},
+@code{ET1}, @code{ES}, @code{ET2}, @code{EA} in @code{IE}; @code{PX0},
+@code{PT0}, @code{PX1}, @code{PT1}, @code{PS}, @code{PT2} in @code{IP};
+@code{T2CON.0} @dots{} @code{T2CON.7}, also spelled @code{CP_RL2},
+@code{C_T2}, @code{TR2}, @code{EXEN2}, @code{TCLK}, @code{RCLK},
+@code{EXF2}, @code{TF2}; and @code{P}, @code{FL} (also spelled @code{F1}),
+@code{OV}, @code{RS0}, @code{RS1}, @code{F0}, @code{AC}, @code{CY} in
+@code{PSW}.  Each stands for the bit's address in the bit space.
+@end table
+
+Any bit of a bit-addressable special function register may also be
+written as the register's name, a dot and the bit number: @samp{PSW.3},
+@samp{IE.7} and @samp{TCON.4} are bit addresses @samp{0xD3}, @samp{0xAF}
+and @samp{0x8C}.
+
+@code{DP0L} and @code{DP0H} are Atmel's spellings of @code{DPL} and
+@code{DPH}.  @code{T2CON}, @code{T2MOD}, @code{RCAP2L}, @code{RCAP2H},
+@code{TL2}, @code{TH2} and the bits @code{T2}, @code{T2EX}, @code{ET2},
+@code{PT2} and those of @code{T2CON} are 8052 names; @code{DP0L},
+@code{DP0H}, @code{DP1L}, @code{DP1H}, @code{SPDR}, @code{SPSR},
+@code{SPCR} and @code{WMCON} are AT89S8252 names.  @option{-mmcu} says
+which of the two groups the assembler knows.
+
+A special function register name, a bit name and @code{AR0} @dots{}
+@code{AR7} may also be used in an ordinary expression, where each evaluates
+to its address.  @samp{.byte ACC} therefore assembles to @code{0xE0}, and
+@samp{.byte CY} to @code{0xD7}.  The names in the first group above
+(@code{A}, @code{DPTR}, @code{@@R0} and the rest) are instruction operands
+only, and are an error in an expression:
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+Error: register `r0' is not valid in an expression
+@end smallexample
+
+@cindex symbols shadowing operand names, MCS-51
+A symbol the program has - one it defines, or declares with @code{.local},
+@code{.global} or the like - is what its name means, whatever built-in name
+it shares, so a part with a different register at an address, or a program
+that wants one of these names for a label, declares its own:
+
+@smallexample
+        .local  WMCON
+        .equ    WMCON, 0x96
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+The declaration has to come before the first use of the name.  A name used
+as the built-in one and defined by the program afterwards would mean two
+different things in one file, and is refused:
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+Error: `ES' is used here as the built-in name and defined later as a
+symbol; declare it with .local first
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+The error is reported at the line that used the built-in name.
+
+@cindex bit of a symbol, MCS-51
+A symbol of the program followed by a dot and a bit number names that bit
+of the byte the symbol names, as @code{B2B ()} does: @code{FLAGS.3} is
+@code{B2B (FLAGS, 3)}.  A name such as @code{LOOP.1} is a symbol of its
+own only where that symbol is already defined; used before its
+definition it means bit 1 of @code{LOOP}, and the definition that follows
+is refused.
+
+@node i51-Modifiers
+@subsection Address Modifiers
+
+@cindex MCS-51 modifiers
+@cindex HIGH, MCS-51
+@cindex LOW, MCS-51
+@cindex B2B, MCS-51
+Three operators take an expression that the assembler may not be able to
+compute, and say which part of it to store.  The keyword is matched without
+regard to case, and white space may separate it from the opening
+parenthesis.  They are operators of the expression language: when their
+operand is a constant they yield a constant, which the rest of the
+expression may use, so @samp{#HIGH (0x1234) + 1} is @code{#0x13}.  When
+their operand is not yet known, the operator becomes a relocation, so it
+has to be the whole operand of an instruction or of a one-byte datum:
+@samp{#HIGH (table) + 1} is an error, not the high byte of
+@code{table + 1}, and so is an operator applied to another one.
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+Error: HIGH(), LOW() and B2B() of a value not known here have to be the
+whole operand
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+Where nothing becomes a relocation - in @code{.equ}, @code{.org},
+@code{.sleb128} or the address of @code{.pcode} - the operand has to be
+known:
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+Error: HIGH() of a value not known here is allowed only as an instruction
+operand or a one-byte datum
+@end smallexample
+
+@table @code
+@item HIGH (@var{expression})
+Bits 8 through 15 of @var{expression}, as an eight-bit value.
+
+@item LOW (@var{expression})
+Bits 0 through 7 of @var{expression}, as an eight-bit value.
+
+@item B2B (@var{expression}, @var{offset})
+The bit address of bit @var{offset} of the byte whose address is
+@var{expression}.  @var{offset} is one of the eight bits of that byte, so
+it runs from 0 through 7.  The byte has to be bit addressable: either one
+of the internal RAM bytes @code{0x20} through @code{0x2F}, for which the
+bit address is
+@w{@code{(@var{expression} - 0x20) * 8 + @var{offset}}}, or a special
+function register whose address is a multiple of eight in @code{0x80}
+through @code{0xF8}, for which the bit address is
+@w{@code{@var{expression} + @var{offset}}}.  A byte that is not bit
+addressable, and an offset past the eighth bit, are refused in all three
+places the fold is made: on a literal, on a symbol the assembler resolves
+itself, and in the linker.
+@end table
+
+@code{HIGH ()} and @code{LOW ()} name a byte.  They may be used on an
+immediate and on a direct address, and on a one-byte data directive:
+
+@smallexample
+        mov     a,#LOW (table)          ; low byte of a code address
+        mov     dph,#HIGH (table)
+        .byte   LOW (value), HIGH (value)   ; a little-endian 16-bit datum
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+They are rejected where a code address or a bit address is expected - the
+target of a jump, a call or a relative branch, and a bit operand - since a
+byte is neither.  On a data directive they are accepted only where the datum
+is one byte wide: @samp{.word HIGH (x)} is an error, because there is no
+two-byte relocation that would carry the request.  Nor are they accepted
+on such a value in @code{.bit}, where the value being assembled is a single
+bit.
+
+@code{B2B ()} names a bit, and is accepted only where a bit address is
+expected, and in a one-byte data directive.  It exists because a byte
+allocated in the bit-addressable part of internal RAM is known to the linker
+by its byte address, while the instruction needs the address of one bit of
+it:
+
+@smallexample
+        .bcomm  FLAGS, 1                ; one bit-addressable byte
+        setb    B2B (FLAGS, 3)          ; bit 3 of it
+@end smallexample
+
+@cindex bit addresses, MCS-51
+A numeric bit address may also be written as a byte address, a dot and a
+bit number 0 through 7, under the same rule about which bytes are bit
+addressable: @code{0x20.3} is bit address @code{0x03} and @code{0x90.5} is
+bit address @code{0x95}.  The number stands alone before the dot:
+@samp{setb 1+0x20.1} is an error.  Like every bit address, it is accepted
+only where an instruction expects a bit: @samp{mov a,0x20.3} is an error,
+not @samp{mov a,0x03}.
+
+@node i51 Byte Order
+@section Byte Order
+
+@cindex byte order, MCS-51
+@cindex endianness, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 byte order
+Everything @command{@value{AS}} writes into a section is stored high byte
+first, and the object files it produces are @code{ELFDATA2MSB}.  There is
+no option to change this.
+
+The MCS-51 has no architectural byte order to inherit: the instruction set
+has no sixteen-bit load or store to data memory at all, so byte order is
+fixed by each construct separately rather than by the machine.  The one
+construct that reaches an object file is the sixteen-bit address inside an
+instruction: @code{ljmp}, @code{lcall} and @code{mov dptr,#@var{data16}}
+all store it high byte first, and @code{.word} follows them, exactly as
+@code{DW} does in Intel's ASM51:
+
+@smallexample
+        .word   0x1234                  @r{assembles to} 12 34
+@end smallexample
+
+A little-endian datum, such as one a run-time routine will load into
+@code{DPTR} a byte at a time, is written per datum out of its two halves:
+
+@smallexample
+        .byte   LOW (value), HIGH (value)
+@end smallexample
+
+@node i51 Floating Point
+@section Floating Point
+
+@cindex floating point, MCS-51 (@sc{ieee})
+@cindex MCS-51 floating point (@sc{ieee})
+The MCS-51 has no floating-point instructions.  @code{.float} and
+@code{.single} assemble @sc{ieee} single precision numbers and
+@code{.double} assembles @sc{ieee} double precision numbers, each stored
+high byte first like every other datum.
+
+@node i51 Memory Spaces
+@section Memory Spaces
+
+@cindex memory spaces, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 memory spaces
+The 8051 is a Harvard machine with several address spaces that have nothing
+to do with one another: an address is meaningless until you know which
+space it belongs to, and two objects in different spaces may perfectly well
+have the same address.  @command{@value{AS}} gives each space its own
+sections, so that a symbol carries its space with it.  Each space has a
+directive that switches to its initialized section, most have one that
+switches to its uninitialized section, and most have a form of @code{.comm}
+that declares a common symbol in it.
+
+Every one of these section directives takes an optional subsection number,
+like @code{.text} and @code{.data} do (@pxref{Sub-Sections}).
+
+@table @asis
+@item Code
+@code{.text}.  Read by @code{movc} and reached by the jump and call
+instructions.
+
+@item Register area, internal RAM @code{0x00} to @code{0x1F}
+@code{.rdata}, @code{.rbss} and @code{.rcomm}.  This is where the four
+register banks live; the @code{.regbank} space below reserves the ones in
+use, and bank 0 always.
+
+@item Bit-addressable bytes, internal RAM @code{0x20} to @code{0x2F}
+@code{.bdata}, @code{.bbss} and @code{.bcomm}.  Bytes here have bit
+addresses as well as byte addresses; @code{B2B ()} converts between the
+two.
+
+@item The bit space
+@code{.bitdata}, @code{.bitbss} and @code{.bitcomm}.  Addresses in this
+space are bit numbers, not byte numbers, and the storage comes out of the
+bit-addressable bytes: eight bits occupy one of those bytes.  A datum here
+is one byte of section contents per bit, which is what @code{.bit}
+assembles.
+
+@item Directly addressable internal RAM
+@code{.data} and @code{.bss}, the ordinary sections, and @code{.comm}.  On
+this target they hold the internal RAM that is reachable by a direct
+address and is not part of the register area or of the bit-addressable
+bytes.
+
+@item Indirectly addressable internal RAM
+@code{.idata}, @code{.ibss} and @code{.icomm}.  Reachable only through
+@code{@@R0} and @code{@@R1}.
+
+@item External RAM
+@code{.xdata}, @code{.xbss} and @code{.xcomm}.  Reachable through
+@code{movx}, sixteen bits of address.
+
+@item A second external space
+@code{.edata}, @code{.ebss} and @code{.ecomm}.  Also reached by
+@code{movx}; on parts that have on-chip memory in a separate @code{movx}
+space, this is where it goes.
+
+@item On-chip EEPROM
+@code{.eeprom}.  Also reached by @code{movx}.  It has no uninitialized
+section and no common form.
+
+@item Register banks
+@code{.regbank}.  No directive switches to it.  When the source uses
+@code{.using}, the assembler emits a common symbol @code{__RB__} in this
+space, sized to reach the end of the highest bank selected, so that the
+link reserves the register area the program uses.
+@end table
+
+@cindex commons, MCS-51
+Every one of the @code{.@var{x}comm} directives spells its space, so the
+linker can tell one declaration from another.  Because the spaces are
+disjoint, a common symbol declared in two of them cannot be arbitrated by
+size the way ordinary commons are, nor can a common of one space be
+satisfied by a definition in another, and the link is refused instead:
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+a.o: symbol `BUF' is in external RAM (.xbss) here
+     and in indirectly addressed internal RAM (.ibss) in b.o
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+Two memory-space commons of one name have to be of one directive: a
+@code{.bcomm} and an @code{.rcomm} of one name are refused although both
+are internal RAM.  A plain @code{.comm} is internal RAM: it may meet a
+common or a definition of any part of internal RAM, and is refused against
+any other space.  Within one file, a name declared a common of one space
+and then of another is an error.
+
+@cindex initialized data in RAM, MCS-51
+@cindex stack, MCS-51
+The RAM of an MCS-51 starts undefined, and the linker script this port
+installs loads nothing into it: the RAM spaces are emitted at their true
+addresses but are not part of the program image.  Bytes a program writes
+into a RAM section - including @code{.bit 1} in @code{.bitdata}, and a
+byte a relocation fills in - would therefore vanish, and the linker
+refuses them.  A zero byte is accepted as a reservation; it does not make
+the RAM zero at reset.  A variable that needs an initial value is
+reserved with @code{.space} and set from code.  The check applies only
+where the output section is not loaded, so a linker script that loads a
+RAM space for a program that copies it into place is not affected.
+@code{.eeprom} is not checked: its contents are what a programmer writes
+into the part.  The default script does not load it either, so an image of
+it is made by loading it on the way out:
+
+@smallexample
+objcopy -O ihex -j .eeprom --set-section-flags .eeprom=alloc,load \
+        prog.elf eeprom.hex
+@end smallexample
+
+The script always reserves register bank 0, which is in use from reset,
+keeps @code{__STACK_SIZE} bytes of internal RAM free above the data for
+the stack (16 by default; @code{STACK} is the first of them), and checks
+the internal RAM against @code{__IRAM_SIZE} (@code{0x100}; give
+@code{0x80} for a part with 128 bytes) and the EEPROM against
+@code{__EEPROM_SIZE}, which is @code{0x10000} unless set and so limits
+nothing: give the part's size, @code{0x800} for the AT89S8252.  Each of
+the three can be set with @option{--defsym}; a program that needs all of
+its internal RAM for data and keeps no stack there links with
+@samp{--defsym __STACK_SIZE=0}.
+
+A linker script that places several spaces at the same load address, as one
+that gives each its own @code{MEMORY} region at origin 0 does, needs
+@option{--no-check-sections}, and its image has to be extracted one space at
+a time, with @samp{objcopy -j .text} and @samp{objcopy -j .eeprom}: a copy of
+the whole file writes the spaces over one another.
+
+@command{nm} shows the symbols of the RAM spaces of a linked image as data,
+@samp{d} or @samp{D}, or as uninitialized data, @samp{b} or @samp{B}, in a
+space that holds nothing else, except that the generic symbol classes take
+@code{.idata} for the import section of a PE file and show its symbols as
+@samp{i}, or @samp{I} for a global one.
+@command{size} counts the program image, which holds the code space only;
+@samp{size -A} lists every space.
+
+@cindex address spaces of relocations, MCS-51
+The linker also refuses an operand that names the wrong space: a jump or
+call to a symbol in data memory, a direct address naming external RAM, the
+bit space or code, and a bit address naming a byte.  A direct address
+naming a symbol of the internal RAM placed at @code{0x80} or above is
+refused too, since a direct address there names a special function
+register.
+
+@node i51 Directives
+@section MCS-51 Machine Directives
+
+@cindex machine directives, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 machine directives
+The directives below are specific to the MCS-51, or behave in a way
+specific to it.
+
+Each memory-space directive takes an optional subsection number, and each
+common directive takes a symbol name, a length in the units of its space,
+and an optional alignment in bytes, a power of two.  A common is at most
+@code{0xFFFF} units long (a longer one draws a warning and is not
+declared), and a memory-space common may ask for an
+alignment of at most @code{0x10000}.  A common that gives no alignment is
+aligned to a byte.
+
+@cindex common alignment, MCS-51
+@cindex ABI generation, MCS-51
+The alignment is recorded in the symbol's @code{st_value}, which is where
+@sc{elf} keeps the alignment of a common symbol, and the linker places the
+symbol at it.  The low four bits of the object's @code{e_flags} say that
+@code{st_value} means this: they are the generation of the ABI, 1 for
+objects this assembler writes.  In an object of generation 0 the field is
+not an alignment, and the linker places those commons at the next free
+byte.  A linker refuses an object of a generation newer than it knows.  A
+linker that predates the field also predates @code{R_I51_16_CODE} and
+@code{R_I51_32}, and refuses the objects this assembler writes.
+
+@table @code
+@cindex @code{bbss} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bbss [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.bbss}, the uninitialized section of the bit-addressable
+internal RAM bytes.
+
+@cindex @code{bcomm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{bcommon} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bcomm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .bcommon @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare a common symbol of @var{length} bytes in the bit-addressable
+internal RAM bytes.
+
+@cindex @code{bdata} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bdata [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.bdata}, the initialized section of the bit-addressable
+internal RAM bytes.
+
+@cindex @code{bit} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bit @var{expressions}
+Assemble one byte for each of the comma-separated @var{expressions}, each
+of which must be the constant 0 or 1: in the bit space one byte of section contents
+describes one bit.  The default linker script loads nothing into the bit
+space, so it refuses a @code{.bit 1} there (@pxref{i51 Memory Spaces}).
+@code{HIGH ()} and @code{LOW ()} of a value not known here are not
+accepted.
+
+@cindex @code{bitbss} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bitbss [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.bitbss}, the uninitialized section of the bit space.
+
+@cindex @code{bitcomm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{bitcommon} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bitcomm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .bitcommon @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare a common symbol of @var{length} bits in the bit space.  This is not
+the same as @code{.bcomm}, which reserves whole bytes.
+
+@cindex @code{bitdata} directive, MCS-51
+@item .bitdata [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.bitdata}, the initialized section of the bit space.
+
+@cindex @code{comm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{common} directive, MCS-51
+@item .comm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .common @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare an ordinary common symbol of @var{length} bytes in internal RAM;
+the linker places it with @code{.data} and @code{.bss}.
+
+A symbol declared @code{.local} before any of the common directives is not
+a common: it is allocated in this file, in the uninitialized section of the
+directive's space, the way @code{.lcomm} allocates in @code{.bss}.  Each
+file that does this has its own copy.
+
+@cindex @code{ebss} directive, MCS-51
+@item .ebss [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.ebss}, the uninitialized section of the second external
+space.
+
+@cindex @code{ecomm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{ecommon} directive, MCS-51
+@item .ecomm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .ecommon @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare a common symbol of @var{length} bytes in the second external space.
+
+@cindex @code{edata} directive, MCS-51
+@item .edata [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.edata}, the initialized section of the second external
+space.
+
+@cindex @code{eeprom} directive, MCS-51
+@item .eeprom [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.eeprom}, the on-chip EEPROM section.
+
+@cindex @code{ibss} directive, MCS-51
+@item .ibss [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.ibss}, the uninitialized section of the indirectly
+addressable internal RAM.
+
+@cindex @code{icomm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{icommon} directive, MCS-51
+@item .icomm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .icommon @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare a common symbol of @var{length} bytes in the indirectly addressable
+internal RAM.
+
+@cindex @code{idata} directive, MCS-51
+@item .idata [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.idata}, the initialized section of the indirectly
+addressable internal RAM.
+
+@cindex @code{pcode} directive, MCS-51
+@item .pcode @var{address} [@var{operand}[, @var{operand}[, @var{operand}]]]
+Assemble one entry of a byte-coded call format.  @var{address} is the code
+address to execute; it has
+thirteen bits, so it must be at least @code{0x100} and at most
+@code{0x1FFF}.  It is one term - a number, a name, @code{HIGH ()},
+@code{LOW ()} or @code{B2B ()} of something, or an expression in
+parentheses - and the first operand starts where that term ends, after
+white space, so it may begin with a sign or a parenthesis; the operands
+are separated from each other by commas.  Up to three
+operands follow, each an expression optionally prefixed by a keyword,
+matched without regard to case, that says how much of it to store and in
+which order:
+
+@table @code
+@item #WORD
+@itemx WORD
+@itemx #
+Both bytes, high byte first.  This is also what an unprefixed operand
+means.
+@item #BYTE
+@itemx BYTE
+The low byte only.
+@item #SWAP
+@itemx SWAP
+Both bytes, with the entry's swap flag set.
+@item #SHL8
+@itemx SHL8
+The low byte, flagged as the high half of a word.
+@item @@WORD
+@itemx @@
+One byte, marked indirect.
+@item @@BYTE
+One byte, marked indirect, stored decremented by one.
+@item @@SWAP
+One byte, marked indirect, with the swap flag set.
+@end table
+
+@noindent
+The entry is two bytes of execution address and flags, one byte of the
+remaining flags, and then the operand bytes.  A byte of a constant operand
+that is zero is left out and the flags record its absence, so an entry is
+only as long as it needs to be; @code{@@BYTE 1} is stored as a zero byte,
+since it is the operand that is not zero.  A keyword without @samp{#} or
+@samp{@@} is the symbol of that name where the program has one.
+
+@smallexample
+        .pcode  pc2x PKT_DEST, arpgwreq, #BYTE sizeofarpgwreq
+@end smallexample
+
+@cindex @code{rbss} directive, MCS-51
+@item .rbss [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.rbss}, the uninitialized section of the register area.
+
+@cindex @code{rcomm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{rcommon} directive, MCS-51
+@item .rcomm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .rcommon @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare a common symbol of @var{length} bytes in the register area.
+
+@cindex @code{rdata} directive, MCS-51
+@item .rdata [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.rdata}, the initialized section of the register area.
+
+@cindex @code{using} directive, MCS-51
+@item .using @var{bank}
+Select register bank @var{bank}, an absolute expression whose value must be
+0, 1, 2 or 3.  This fixes
+the byte addresses that @code{AR0} @dots{} @code{AR7} stand for:
+@code{AR@var{n}} becomes @code{0x00}, @code{0x08}, @code{0x10} or
+@code{0x18} plus @var{n}, according to the bank.  Using one of those names
+before any @code{.using} is an error.  The selection holds until the next
+@code{.using}.
+
+Source that uses @code{.using} at all also gets a common symbol
+@code{__RB__} in the @code{.regbank} space, sized to reach the end of the
+highest bank any of its files selected, so that the link reserves the
+register area the program uses.  The name is the assembler's; a program
+that defines @code{__RB__} itself is refused.
+
+@cindex @code{xbss} directive, MCS-51
+@item .xbss [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.xbss}, the uninitialized section of external RAM.
+
+@cindex @code{xcomm} directive, MCS-51
+@cindex @code{xcommon} directive, MCS-51
+@item .xcomm @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+@itemx .xcommon @var{symbol}, @var{length}[, @var{alignment}]
+Declare a common symbol of @var{length} bytes in external RAM.
+
+@cindex @code{xdata} directive, MCS-51
+@item .xdata [@var{subsection}]
+Switch to @code{.xdata}, the initialized section of external RAM.
+@end table
+
+@cindex @code{local} directive, MCS-51
+@code{.local} is the ELF directive, and marks each of its names local.  It
+also makes each of them a symbol of the program, which then takes the place
+of a built-in operand name of the same spelling (@pxref{i51-Regs}), and
+makes a common directive that follows allocate the symbol in this file.
+
+@cindex @code{$}, MCS-51
+@samp{$} separates statements on one line, so it is neither a character of
+a name nor the mark of a local label.
+
+@cindex @code{ds} directive, MCS-51
+@code{.ds} is the generic directive, which reserves its count of two-byte
+units: @samp{.ds 3} reserves six bytes.  This differs from ASEM-51 and
+@command{sdas8051}, where it reserves bytes; @code{.ds.b} is the form that
+reserves bytes.
+
+@node i51 Opcodes
+@section Opcodes
+
+@cindex MCS-51 opcodes
+@cindex opcodes for MCS-51
+@command{@value{AS}} implements the standard MCS-51 instruction set:
+
+@c doctruth: mnemonics
+@smallexample
+acall  add    addc   ajmp   anl    cjne   clr    cpl
+da     dec    div    djnz   inc    jb     jbc    jc
+jmp    jnb    jnc    jnz    jz     lcall  ljmp   mov
+movc   movx   mul    nop    orl    pop    push   ret
+reti   rl     rlc    rr     rrc    setb   sjmp   subb
+swap   xch    xchd   xrl
+@end smallexample
+
+For the instruction set itself, see Intel's @cite{MCS-51 Microcontroller
+Family User's Manual}.  An operand form the instruction set does not have is
+an error, as is anything after the last operand.
+
+@cindex generic jump and call, MCS-51
+@code{jmp} with a code address, and @code{call}, are the generic jump and
+call of MCS-51 assembly; nothing is relaxed, so they assemble to the long
+forms @code{ljmp} and @code{lcall}, which reach the whole code space.
+@samp{jmp @@a+dptr} is the indirect jump.
+
+Nothing is relaxed, so the ranges of the branch instructions are the
+machine's own.  @code{sjmp} and the conditional branches take a signed
+eight-bit displacement, so their target must lie within @minus{}128 to 127
+bytes of the instruction that follows.  @code{acall} and @code{ajmp} encode
+only the low eleven bits of the target and take the rest from the address
+of the following instruction, so the target has to be in the same 2K page
+as that address.  Both depend on where the instruction ends up, so a branch
+to a target in this section is resolved by the assembler, and any other -
+to a constant address, to another section, or to a weak symbol, which a
+strong definition elsewhere may replace - by the linker, which reports a
+target out of range as an overflow on @code{R_I51_7_PCREL} or
+@code{R_I51_11}.  An @code{acall} or @code{ajmp} always goes to the linker.
+@code{ljmp} and @code{lcall} reach the whole code space.
+
+@cindex direct addressing, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 direct addressing
+A direct operand is one byte of address in the internal RAM and special
+function register space, where @code{0x80} through @code{0xFF} names the
+special function registers.  The upper half of the internal RAM answers to
+those same addresses and only @code{@@R0} and @code{@@R1} reach it, so an
+object there cannot be named by a direct operand.  The assembler says so
+where it can see that this is what a program has done, which is when the
+operand is a symbol the assembler places itself and whose offset in its own
+memory space is @code{0x80} or more:
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+Error: `BUF' is at 0x90, which a direct address cannot reach: 0x80 and
+above is the special function register page, and RAM there is reachable
+only through @@R0/@@R1
+@end smallexample
+
+@noindent
+The check is made as the relocations are applied, which is where the
+fragments have addresses, so an object allocated with @code{.ds} is
+diagnosed like any other.  A symbol the linker places - one this file leaves
+undefined, or a common - has no address here, and is left to the linker.
+
+A number is never refused, at any value from @code{0x00} to @code{0xFF}.  An absolute value of
+@code{0x80} or above is how a special function register is written, whether
+spelled @code{ACC}, given a name with @code{.equ} or written out, and
+nothing tells that apart from an object a program has placed at the same
+address by hand.  An immediate operand is not refused either:
+@samp{mov r0,#@var{object}} is how an object in the upper RAM is reached,
+through @code{@@R0} afterwards.
+
+@node i51 Relocations
+@section Relocations
+
+@cindex relocations, MCS-51
+@cindex MCS-51 relocations
+These are the relocation names that appear in @command{readelf} output, in
+linker diagnostics, and as the @var{reloc_name} argument of @code{.reloc}
+(@pxref{Reloc,,@code{.reloc}}).
+
+@table @code
+@item R_I51_NONE
+No relocation.
+
+@item R_I51_8
+An eight-bit value whose operand kind is not recorded: a byte of data from
+@code{.byte}, or a byte operand of @code{.pcode}.  The value is added to
+the byte at the place.  The assembler and the linker accept @minus{}255
+through 255, as for any one-byte datum.
+
+@item R_I51_8_DIR
+A direct address: one byte naming the internal RAM and special function
+register space.  That space runs @code{0x00} through @code{0xFF}, so the
+linker refuses a value outside it rather than storing the low byte of
+something else.
+
+@item R_I51_8_IMM
+An eight-bit immediate.  The instruction that reads it decides whether the
+byte is signed, so the linker accepts @minus{}128 through 255 and refuses
+anything else.
+
+@item R_I51_L
+@itemx R_I51_H
+The low and the high byte of a sixteen-bit value, from @code{LOW ()} and
+@code{HIGH ()}.
+
+@item R_I51_8_BIT
+A bit address, in the bit operand of @code{setb}, @code{clr}, @code{cpl},
+@code{jb}, @code{jnb}, @code{jbc}, @code{mov}, @code{anl} or @code{orl}.
+
+@item R_I51_8_B2B
+A bit address that the linker has to compute from the byte address of the
+symbol, from @code{B2B ()}.  The byte being relocated holds the bit offset
+until then.
+
+@item R_I51_7_PCREL
+The displacement of a relative branch: one signed byte, measured from the
+instruction that follows.
+
+@item R_I51_11
+The target of @code{acall} or @code{ajmp}.  Eleven bits, three of which sit
+in the opcode byte and eight in the byte after it; the high five bits of
+the address are not stored and have to match those of the following
+instruction.
+
+@item R_I51_16
+A sixteen-bit number: the operand of @code{mov dptr,#@var{data16}}, and a
+symbol in @code{.word}.  The assembler and the linker accept
+@minus{}65535 through 65535, as for any two-byte datum.
+
+@item R_I51_16_CODE
+A code address: the operand of @code{ljmp} and @code{lcall}, 0 through
+@code{0xFFFF}.
+
+@item R_I51_32
+A thirty-two-bit datum: a symbol in @code{.long}, and the addresses in
+debugging information.
+
+@item R_I51_13_PCODE
+The execution address of a @code{.pcode} entry.  Thirteen bits in a
+sixteen-bit word whose top three bits are flags and are preserved.
+@end table
+
+Two further names, @code{R_I51_R1} and @code{R_I51_R3}, are reserved; the
+assembler never generates them.
+
+One-, two- and four-byte data can carry a relocation.  A symbol in a wider
+data directive is refused:
+
+@c doctruth: diagnostic
+@smallexample
+        .quad   sym        @r{Error: cannot relocate 8 bytes of data}
+@end smallexample
